{"step_index":742,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T18:33:46Z","thinking":"**Focusing on proof strategy**\n\nThe goal is to prove `(nontrivial) ⇒ (radius + 1) ≤ roman_domination_number)` for all graphs, confirmed beyond any doubt. The refutation should remain with the `sorry`. Reviewing available Lean lemmas to determine the best approach for the proof.\n\n\n**Considering proof implementation strategy**\n\nThe core of the problem is proving the inequality involving radius and the Roman domination number. Reviewing available Lean libraries for graph theory lemmas related to these concepts is the next step. I should search specifically for lemmas relating the radius to the domination number, or related invariants.\n\n\n**Analyzing definitions to establish approach**\n\nThe definitions of `romanDominationNumber` and `radius` are being carefully examined for their relationship. The `romanDominationNumber` is the minimum of `romanWeight` over all Roman dominating functions, while `radius` is based on the minimum eccentricity over all vertices.  Now, the plan is to connect these two definitions through the conditions of the `Candidate` statement.\n\n\n**Evaluating relevant lemmas**\n\nExamining `le_romanDominationNumber` reveals a useful structure: it provides a lower bound on `romanDominationNumber` based on the weight of any Roman dominating function. `radius_le_eccentricity` shows that `radius` is always less than or equal to `eccentricity`. This could be useful if a connection between eccentricity and roman domination number is found. The goal now is to see if any such connection exists or could be constructed to guide the proof attempt.\n\n\n**Exploring implications of graph properties**\n\nThe lemma `minUpTo_le` is being investigated to determine if it provides useful bounds given the assumption of `HasRadius G`. The cardinality of the vertex set and the nontriviality condition `IsNontrivialClass G` are also being considered. Specifically, the fact that `Fintype.card V ≥ 2` because of the `IsNontrivialClass G` constraint on the graph is being kept in mind.\n\nConsidered the definition of `HasRadius G`, which implies that for any vertex v, the ball within G with radius `Fintype.card V` is the entire vertex set `univ`. Now testing `minUpTo_le` with `k = Fintype.card V`. Since this should give the ball being the entire set which is crucial for using `minUpTo_le`.\n\n\n**Refining inequality constraints**\n\nObserved that eccentricity is bounded by the number of vertices, implying radius is also bounded by the number of vertices. Considered whether a tighter bound, `radius ≤ Fintype.card V - 1`, is necessary.\n\nFurther exploration shows the tighter bound might be unnecessary. If the Roman weight is greater than or equal to the number of vertices plus one, then the desired inequality holds even with the weaker bound. This simplifies the proof strategy by removing the need to prove a tighter radius bound. Now assessing the case when the roman weight is less than or equal to the number of vertices.\n\nExamined the implications when the Roman weight is less than or equal to the number of vertices. Decomposed the Roman weight in terms of the number of vertices, single dominated vertices, and double dominated vertices. The inequality `romanWeight ≤ |V|` implies a relationship between the sizes of different vertex sets.\n\n\n**Analyzing the implication of `romanWeight` bounds**\n\nExamined the case where a Roman dominating function has `|V_2| = 0`. This forces `|V_0| = 0` and thus, `romanWeight f = |V|`. Now focused on the question `radius + 1 ≤ |V|`.\n\nThe definition of `HasRadius` and `eccentricity` were considered, particularly how `eccentricity` is the result of a `minUpTo` search on the size of the ball. The key is to see that `ballWithin G (k-1) v = univ` can never be false while `ballWithin G k v = univ` is true.\n\nSince `ballWithin G k v` expands monotonically and is eventually equal to `univ`, it must reach `univ` at some step. This happens because the ball starts from a single vertex and at each step `k`, `ballWithin G k v ⊆ ballWithin G (k+1) v`. If at some step k, the ball stops growing, it never expands. Therefore, the ball will strictly grow until the full vertex set is reached.\n\n\n**Establishing the fundamental radius bound**\n\nThe increasing nature of `ballWithin` is key. Its size strictly increases until it stabilizes at or before step n-1, given a graph size of n. This implies that if the ball reaches `univ`, it does so at or before step n-1. Therefore, `eccentricity G v ≤ n - 1` always holds. Consequently, `radius G ≤ n - 1`, and thus, `radius G + 1 ≤ |V|`.\n\n**Connecting bound to `romanWeight` inequality**\n\nA universal bound on radius plus one has been derived: `radius G + 1 ≤ Fintype.card V`. Now, if `romanWeight f ≥ Fintype.card V`, then `radius G + 1 ≤ Fintype.card V ≤ romanWeight f`, directly satisfying the goal for any Roman dominating function with sufficiently large weight.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"view_file","args":{"AbsolutePath":"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/tools/antigravity_handoff.py","EndLine":250,"StartLine":150,"toolAction":"Viewing lint function in antigravity_handoff.py","toolSummary":"View lint function"}}]}
{"step_index":743,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T18:34:01Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-11T20:34:01+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T20:34:01+02:00\nFile Path: `file:///mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/tools/antigravity_handoff.py`\nTotal Lines: 348\nTotal Bytes: 15551\nShowing lines 150 to 250\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n150:     return re.sub(r\"--[^\\n]*\", \" \", src)\n151: \n152: \n153: def _prefix(original: str):\n154:     \"\"\"Everything up to the first theorem: imports, options, `Candidate`.\"\"\"\n155:     i = original.find(\"abbrev Candidate\")\n156:     j = original.find(\"\\n\\ntheorem \", i) if i >= 0 else -1\n157:     return original[:j] if j >= 0 else None\n158: \n159: \n160: def structural_check(original: str, returned: str):\n161:     \"\"\"(ok, reason): may the returned file be compiled as an answer at all?\"\"\"\n162:     orig, ret = _norm(original), _norm(returned)\n163:     pre = _prefix(orig)\n164:     if pre is None:\n165:         return False, \"original challenge is malformed\"\n166:     if not ret.startswith(pre):\n167:         return False, \"statement changed: text above the first theorem differs\"\n168:     rest = ret[len(pre):]\n169:     if rest and not rest.startswith(\"\\n\"):\n170:         return False, \"statement changed: Candidate's last line was extended\"\n171:     code = _strip_comments(rest)\n172:     head = code.lstrip()\n173:     if head and not head.startswith(_CMD_START):\n174:         return False, \"statement changed: Candidate continues past its original end\"\n175:     m = _FORBIDDEN_CMD.search(code)\n176:     if m:\n177:         return False, f\"forbidden command `{m.group(1)}`\"\n178:     m = _FORBIDDEN_TOKEN.search(code)\n179:     if m:\n180:         return False, f\"forbidden construct `{m.group(0)}`\"\n181:     return True, \"\"\n182: \n183: \n184: def lint_text(text: str, original=None):\n185:     \"\"\"(ok, reason) for any Lean file an agent asks to elaborate.\n186: \n187:     A challenge file is held to `structural_check` against its original. Any\n188:     other file (scratch lemmas) gets the same code-execution screen plus an\n189:     import whitelist, since `import` is the other way to pull in code.\n190:     \"\"\"\n191:     if original is not None:\n192:         return structural_check(original, text)\n193:     code = _strip_comments(_norm(text))\n194:     for m in re.finditer(r\"(?m)^\\s*import\\s+(\\S+)\", code):\n195:         if m.group(1) not in _ALLOWED_IMPORTS:\n196:             return False, f\"import `{m.group(1)}` is not allowed\"\n197:     m = _FORBIDDEN_CMD.search(code)\n198:     if m:\n199:         return False, f\"forbidden command `{m.group(1)}`\"\n200:     m = _FORBIDDEN_TOKEN.search(code)\n201:     if m:\n202:         return False, f\"forbidden construct `{m.group(0)}`\"\n203:     return True, \"\"\n204: \n205: \n206: def solution_file(returned: str, ident: str, direction: str):\n207:     \"\"\"The returned file prepared to check one direction, or None.\n208: \n209:     The other direction's untouched `sorry` block is removed, and a declaration\n210:     is appended that forces Lean to confirm the target has exactly the type\n211:     asked for — `_root_`-qualified, so a same-named `Candidate` or target in some\n212:     other namespace cannot stand in for it.\n213:     \"\"\"\n214:     ret = _norm(returned)\n215:     other = \"refute\" if direction == \"prove\" else \"prove\"\n216:     header = {\"prove\": rf\"(?m)^theorem\\s+prove_{ident}\\s*:\\s*Candidate\\s*:=\",\n217:               \"refute\": rf\"(?m)^theorem\\s+refute_{ident}\\s*:\\s*¬\\s*Candidate\\s*:=\"}\n218:     pristine = {\"prove\": f\"theorem prove_{ident} : Candidate := by\\n  sorry\",\n219:                 \"refute\": f\"theorem refute_{ident} : ¬ Candidate := by\\n  sorry\"}\n220:     if not re.search(header[direction], _strip_comments(ret)):\n221:         return None\n222:     if pristine[direction] in ret:\n223:         return None\n224:     body = ret.replace(pristine[other], \"\")\n225:     ty = \"_root_.Candidate\" if direction == \"prove\" else \"¬ _root_.Candidate\"\n226:     return (body.rstrip() + \"\\n\\n-- verifier: the target must have exactly this type\\n\"\n227:             f\"theorem agf_verify_{direction} : {ty} := _root_.{direction}_{ident}\\n\")\n228: \n229: \n230: def do_verify(args) -> int:\n231:     from pipeline.theorem_prover import LeanSubprocessProver\n232:     from tools.prover_eval.w16 import is_timeout\n233: \n234:     index, src = _index(args.benchmark, args.partition)\n235:     truth = {}\n236:     gt = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(args.dir.rstrip(\"/\")), \".ground_truth.json\")\n237:     if os.path.exists(gt):\n238:         truth = json.load(open(gt))\n239: \n240:     CONFIG.lean_timeout_s = args.timeout\n241:     lean = LeanSubprocessProver(CONFIG)\n242:     if not lean._available:\n243:         print(\"ERROR: no Lean available — cannot verify.\")\n244:         return 2\n245: \n246:     results, counts = [], collections.Counter()\n247:     for item in index:\n248:         ident, fname = item[\"id\"], item[\"file\"]\n249:         path = os.path.join(args.dir, fname)\n250:         if not os.path.exists(path):\nThe above content does NOT show the entire file contents. If you need to view any lines of the file which were not shown to complete your task, call this tool again to view those lines.\n"}
{"step_index":744,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T18:34:01Z","thinking":"**Defining theorem regex patterns**\n\nThe code defines regex patterns for identifying theorem statements, differentiating between \"prove\" and \"refute\" directions based on the input string and using identifiers. It also establishes pristine theorem templates for both proof and refutation cases, ensuring that the theorem statements and negation are accurately formed.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"view_file","args":{"AbsolutePath":"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/tools/antigravity_handoff.py","EndLine":150,"StartLine":100,"toolAction":"Viewing forbidden tokens in antigravity_handoff.py","toolSummary":"View forbidden tokens"}}]}
{"step_index":745,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T18:34:04Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-11T20:34:04+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T20:34:04+02:00\nFile Path: `file:///mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/tools/antigravity_handoff.py`\nTotal Lines: 348\nTotal Bytes: 15551\nShowing lines 100 to 150\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n100: \n101: \n102: # ── admissibility ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n103: #\n104: # An answer is compiled as the *returned file*, helper lemmas included — pasting\n105: # only the proof body back into the original, as the model campaign did, drops\n106: # every lemma a real proof needs and turns a correct proof into \"unresolved\".\n107: #\n108: # Compiling the returned file is only sound if nothing in it can change what is\n109: # being proved. The checks below close the routes that exist in Lean for that:\n110: # editing `Candidate` or anything it elaborates against; extending its term past\n111: # where it ended (a term does not stop at a newline); re-declaring `Candidate` or\n112: # the target inside a namespace; adding `variable` hypotheses the target silently\n113: # absorbs; redefining the tokens `Candidate` parses as; and bypassing the kernel.\n114: # The target's type is then re-checked by Lean itself, and the axiom audit rejects\n115: # `sorryAx` and anything non-standard.\n116: \n117: _CMD_START = (\"theorem\", \"lemma\", \"def\", \"abbrev\", \"example\", \"instance\", \"open\",\n118:               \"set_option\", \"attribute\", \"noncomputable\", \"private\", \"protected\",\n119:               \"@[\", \"#check\", \"#print\", \"structure\", \"inductive\", \"class\")\n120: \n121: _FORBIDDEN_CMD = re.compile(\n122:     r\"(?m)^\\s*(?:(?:private|protected|noncomputable|local|scoped)\\s+)*\"\n123:     r\"(namespace|section|variable|universe|notation\\w*|infixl?|infixr|prefix|postfix|\"\n124:     r\"macro\\w*|syntax\\w*|declare_syntax_cat|elab\\w*|initialize|builtin_initialize|\"\n125:     r\"run_cmd|run_elab|run_meta)\\b\")\n126: \n127: # Lean elaboration can run arbitrary code: `#eval IO.Process.run …`, `run_cmd`,\n128: # a user `elab`, an `@[init]` hook. Agents are only allowed to run the checker,\n129: # and the verifier compiles their files with full user privileges, so anything\n130: # that reaches IO or the metaprogramming API is refused *before* Lean sees the\n131: # file — that refusal is what makes \"only check.sh may run\" an actual boundary.\n132: _FORBIDDEN_TOKEN = re.compile(\n133:     r\"\\b(native_decide|ofReduceBool|trustCompiler|skipKernelTC|implemented_by|\"\n134:     r\"extern|axiom|unsafe\\w*|run_tac|run_cmd|run_elab|run_meta|addDecl\\w*|\"\n135:     r\"Lean|\\w*IO|System|Process|MetaM|CoreM|TermElabM|CommandElabM|TacticM|\"\n136:     r\"macro\\w*|elab\\w*|syntax\\w*|notation\\w*|initialize|builtin_initialize)\\b\"\n137:     r\"|#eval|#exit|\\bdebug\\.|@\\[\\s*(?:init|builtin_init|command_elab|term_elab|\"\n138:     r\"tactic|extern|implemented_by)\")\n139: \n140: _ALLOWED_IMPORTS = {\"Mathlib\", \"LeanProject.GraphInvariantsComputable\",\n141:                     \"LeanProject.GraphInvariants\"}\n142: \n143: \n144: def _norm(text: str) -> str:\n145:     return \"\\n\".join(line.rstrip() for line in text.replace(\"\\r\\n\", \"\\n\").split(\"\\n\"))\n146: \n147: \n148: def _strip_comments(src: str) -> str:\n149:     src = re.sub(r\"/-.*?-/\", \" \", src, flags=re.S)\n150:     return re.sub(r\"--[^\\n]*\", \" \", src)\nThe above content does NOT show the entire file contents. If you need to view any lines of the file which were not shown to complete your task, call this tool again to view those lines.\n"}
